tcome_telemetry}.py.\\n\\nDECLARED CONDITIONS CHECKED AGAINST THE CODE (this payload's share; none is false)\\n- C5: check_arbitration_kernel.py:310 has \\\".claude/hooks/_lib/agent_spawn/**/*\\\" and :332 has \\\".claude/hooks/_lib/model_currency.py\\\". spool_writer.py is not a kernel path, so _spool_writer_parts not being kernel is no gap. The reality ledger is script-side.\\n- C7: the moved code binds into its facades. check_agent_spawn.py:126-139, 586, 615 use _audit_emit_parts.install(package=\\\"_lib.agent_spawn\\\"), and :640-647 use agent_spawn.bind. spool_writer.py:588-590 and reality-ledger.py:81-83 bind the same way. I listed every global the moved functions look up, and each one exists in its facade: the spool path, lock and journal helpers, the state flags, DrainStats/JournalReconciliation, the _FA_*/_OVERLAP_* constants (prompt_rules), _audit_log_path/_parse_event_ts (outcome_telemetry), and _REPO_ROOT/redact_secrets in the reality-ledger facade. The binding is what supplies these: `from .constants import *` alone would not, because those names are absent from the constants __all__. Every name in each part's __all__ is defined in that part.\\n- C8: spool_writer.py:528-530 reads CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT, which _python-hook.sh:410 exports. lifecycle.py:346-350 (_atexit_drain) and _signal_drain_handler then skip both drain_now and the journal flush. That matches ADR-055-AMEND-4 §4.2. The signal handler now chains to the previous disposition even during spool_append; the v1.4.2-era code swallowed the signal. That is an improvement.\\n- C9: agent_frontmatter.py:79-82 and 256-263, with model_currency.py:16 and 47-52, admit families: claude-opus-4..9, gpt-5..9, o3..9, -mini variants and dated ids. Only the bare alias claude-opus-4-1 is refused, so claude-opus-4-1-<date> passes. The CHANGELOG declares this and the stale docstring.\\n- Fail direction: entrypoint.py allows on a component import error, and _load_spawn_part prints {} and exits 0 (open on an infrastructure fault). If model_currency raises ImportError, the closed list stays strict. Consistent with the doctrine.\\n- Python 3.9 / stdlib only: OK. Module-level annotations such as Tuple[...] in advisories.py and model_routing.py, where typing is not imported, are never evaluated under `from __future__ import annotations`.\\n\\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\\nP1 — .claude/hooks/_lib/agent_spawn/file_assignment.py:327-379 (_recent_file_assignments reads only the canonical log, :340), together with _spool_writer_parts/lifecycle.py:346-350 and spool_writer.py:528-530.\\nFailure scenario: under CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1 every audit event goes to the per-PID spool (_audit_emit_parts/writer.py:579-592), and the hook exits without draining.\\n- An opportunistic drain fires only when a process's own spool is more than 100 ms old at its next emit. Each drain moves at most the K_MAX=100 oldest entries.\\n- So check_agent_spawn (pid A) records spawn_file_assignment_recorded for \\\"CAN edit: x.py\\\" in state/audit-spool.A.jsonl, and the record stays there.\\n- The next sequential spawn (pid B) with the same path tails audit-log.jsonl only. It finds no reservation, so CEO_SPAWN_OVERLAP_GUARD=1 does not block the clobber.\\n- Before this train, pid A's atexit drain_now(force=True) appended the record before exiting.\\n- The guard is opt-in, so this fails open, but silently. Condition 8 and the Known-open declare pending spool work and unproven later collection. They do not declare that in-session readers of the canonical log go blind, including this governance rail and outcome_telemetry._tail_recent_rendered.\\nMinimal fix: have _recent_file_assignments also scan state/audit-spool.*.jsonl and *.draining.* body lines (same event JSON, same window filter). Alternatively, write spawn_file_assignment_recorded straight to the canonical log, bypassing the spool. Then declare the lag for other canonical-log readers.\\n\\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\\n1. _spool_writer_parts/{writer,prepare,drain,lifecycle}.py:29-31: _HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent resolves to .claude/hooks/_lib, not .claude/hooks. This is identical moved text with different behavior: every process that imports spool_writer gets _lib prepended to sys.path[0]. I found no collision today (checked _lib names against hooks/, scripts/, scripts/*/ and bare third-party imports). It is a latent risk of shadowing and of duplicate module identities (filelock, frontmatter, mcp). Fix: delete the block, since the facade already set sys.path, or use .parent.parent.parent.\\n2. The same four parts carry dead copies of K_MAX, DRAIN_TRIGGER_*, SPOOL_LOCK_TIMEOUT and the module imports (lines ~33-66), plus docstrings that claim to be _lib/spool_writer.py. Editing them has no effect because the functions bind to the facade's values. Fix: remove them.\\n3. drain.py:489-531 (_phase5_split_and_cleanup) rewrites and fsyncs every swept file the cycle did not consume. With bounded exit leaving one spool per hook process and at most 100 entries moved per drain, drain cost grows with the backlog while throughput stays fixed. That cost lands inside whichever hook wins the lock. Fix: cap the Phase-2 sweep to the oldest files needed for K_MAX entries, or skip rewriting untouched files.\\n4. _shipped_path_census/census.py:100-101: on Python 3.9-3.11, ast.parse raises ValueError on NUL bytes, and the except clause misses it, so the census crashes instead of reporting \\\"unproven\\\". Only tests call it. Fix: add ValueError to the except.\\n5. agent_frontmatter.py:132-138 and 208-231 still describe membership in VETO_FLOOR_ALLOWED as the enforcement rule. The CHANGELOG declares this; carry it.\\n6. outcome_telemetry.py (_tail_recent_rendered): has the same lag as the P1. The GOAP outcome can be misfiled as no_render_prior when the render event sits behind a backlog larger than K_MAX. Telemetry only.\\n7. Not verifiable from this tree: scripts/upgrade.sh is withheld. The rewritten facades hard-require the new _lib subpackages; if those are missing, agent-spawn governance and spool writing fail open. The CHANGELOG states that upgrades receive them. Check this in the payload that carries upgrade.sh.\",\"decision\":\"GO-WITH-CONDITIONS\",\"sentence\":\"Declared conditions 5, 7, 8 and 9 hold against this payload's code and no P0 was found, but one undeclared P1 must go into the signed annex: under CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT the spawn overlap guard reads only the canonical log, so it misses reservations still waiting in undrained spools.\"}","structured_output":{"report":"PAYLOAD 12/32. Files: _reality_ledger_parts/{findings,walking}.py, _shipped_path_census/*, _skill_telemetry_router/*, _spool_writer_parts/{__init__,writer,prepare,drain,lifecycle}.py, agent_frontmatter.py, agent_spawn/{__init__,advisories,entrypoint,file_assignment,model_routing,outcome_telemetry}.py.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS CHECKED AGAINST THE CODE (this payload's share; none is false)\n- C5: check_arbitration_kernel.py:310 has \".claude/hooks/_lib/agent_spawn/**/*\" and :332 has \".claude/hooks/_lib/model_currency.py\". spool_writer.py is not a kernel path, so _spool_writer_parts not being kernel is no gap. The reality ledger is script-side.\n- C7: the moved code binds into its facades. check_agent_spawn.py:126-139, 586, 615 use _audit_emit_parts.install(package=\"_lib.agent_spawn\"), and :640-647 use agent_spawn.bind. spool_writer.py:588-590 and reality-ledger.py:81-83 bind the same way. I listed every global the moved functions look up, and each one exists in its facade: the spool path, lock and journal helpers, the state flags, DrainStats/JournalReconciliation, the _FA_*/_OVERLAP_* constants (prompt_rules), _audit_log_path/_parse_event_ts (outcome_telemetry), and _REPO_ROOT/redact_secrets in the reality-ledger facade. The binding is what supplies these: `from .constants import *` alone would not, because those names are absent from the constants __all__. Every name in each part's __all__ is defined in that part.\n- C8